#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82c825b9-fc36-510c-982e-e4596ab2ce24" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Distance to a Circle's Center"}] :question/id #uuid "2b2211b8-cc3f-5dd6-9dc0-1dc1a7f69513" :question/math-academy-id "e-3166" :question/problem "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $4$, a point $C$ that lies on the circle, and a third point $P$ on the plane.\n\n![](images/e6/e6ede881702a8713cf9c878f9a9d7fbdd0bdcb14bcab500d3cf256cc9cc84463.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\n1. $OP \\leq 4$\n2. $OC = 4$\n3. $OP > 4$" :question/worked-solution "The point $C$ lies on the circle itself. So, the distance from $O$ to $C$ is equal to the radius of the circle:\n$OC = 4$.\n\nOn the other hand, the point $P$ lies strictly outside the circle. So, the distance between $P$ and the center $O$ of the circle must be greater than the radius:\n\n$$\nOP > OC = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105558 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa963eb-82a7-56cb-a8b8-f9291adec2d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Length of a Line Segment With Endpoints on a Circle and Passing Through the Center"}] :question/id #uuid "9e6ebec8-4c08-5bc9-8a8c-4c6cedb2bd8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3167" :question/problem "Consider the diagram below. What is the measure of $\\overline{AB}$ if $OQ=3$?\n\n![](images/4e/4e32346cc949b0f17ed219e44d4600abd23a0839a3855fcf76ff4060f322698c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the points $A$, $B$, and $Q$ lie on the circle. From the diagram, the radius of the circle is $r =OQ=3$.\n\nTo find $AB$, we first notice that\n\n$$\nAB = AO + OB\n$$\n\nNow, $\\overline{AO}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both segments between the center of the circle and the circle itself. So, they are both radii of the circle, and because the radius is $3$, we have\n\n$$\nAO=3, \\qquad OB=3\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nAB = AO+OB = 3 + 3= 6\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2401d489-0970-54a8-a70d-9e8613458f54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Line Segments Associated with a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "cb990fba-991a-58c8-a404-54353183903c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3168" :question/problem "![](images/60/606c8bb2352215fe5c34acb78742034df016b8f41c7a6194b700e861f4f35596.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\n1. $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord of the circle\n2. $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter of the circle\n3. $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius of the circle" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a chord.\n- Statement II is true. As we can see, both endpoints $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle and $\\overline{BC}$ passes through the center $O$. Therefore, $\\overline{BC}$ is a diameter.\n- Statement III is true. The segment $\\overline{OC}$ connects a point on the circle with its center. So, $\\overline{OC}$ is a radius.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2401d489-0970-54a8-a70d-9e8613458f54" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Line Segments Associated with a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9b6ece2-c175-5a62-a4c8-d3a7bc1bcb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104681} {:answer/id #uuid "bac5f487-8251-5e3f-a25d-1940879a8ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104682} {:answer/id #uuid "d191d7ec-f08f-53da-9bdf-ef6d460a8ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104683} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1687fe-5277-552d-af56-af2b924dbf53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104684} {:answer/id #uuid "5333d1c5-bab1-5a7b-8948-71d6f629ad61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104685}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f61b895f-2b53-547b-9ce6-170508475559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104680}] :question/id #uuid "36da278f-fa98-5fed-9b0f-aa9974a3b0ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-53527" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f772ad4b54217a479b63a6919a915af7bd8f44013687dae29712bf09624c68b.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true about the diagram above?\n\n1. $AB^(―)$ is a diameter of the circle\n2. $BC^(―)$ is a radius of the circle\n3. $OA^(―)$ is a chord of the circle"}]}
